    "product_id": "811db568-4103-414d-90d4-e654b65f7008",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 151136\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n436 AM MST Thu Oct 15 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Dry weather is expected over the next couple of days with\n  temperatures warming to near to slightly below normal starting\n  Today.\n  \n- Locally windy conditions will impact the western deserts\n  starting Today before spreading across the rest of southern and\n  central Arizona Friday into Saturday.\n\n- Wind advisories have been posted for portions of southern\n  Arizona into the Lower Colorado River Valley on Friday with wind\n  gusts in excess of 40 miles per hour expected.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \nThe low pressure system that brought multiple rounds of showers \nand below normal temperatures earlier this week is now currently \ncentered over Colorado, and continues to move northeastwards. \nMeanwhile, to the west, high pressure is beginning to build, \nbringing clear skies and H5 heights forecasted to reach between \n582-585 dam today. This will swing afternoon high temperatures up \nto 10 degrees higher than yesterday, expect highs in the mid to \nupper 80s across the lower deserts, with a low chance (10-20%) of \nPhoenix reaching 90 degrees. These temperatures, while higher than\nearlier this week, are still right around normal for mid October.\nLow temperatures on the other hand will lag behind with today's \nlow expected to remain below normal in the mid to upper 50s. By \nFriday morning the lows will respond to the building high \npressure, with forecasted lows in the upper 60 to low 70s, or near\n5 degrees above normal. \n\nThe main weather concern will be increasing winds starting today and \nlasting into the weekend as a pressure gradient strengthens as \nthe high pressure builds to the to the region's north. In \naddition to the building high, a cut off low will begin to form \nlate tonight to the region's SW over the Norther Baja peninsula. \nThe strongest breezes during the day will be between 25-35 mph and\nfocused across northern AZ and across portions of southeast CA, \nincluding the lower Colorado River Valley. By late tonight winds \nacross theses areas will begin to increase to between 35-45 mph, \nin response a Wind Advisory will begin and last through late \nFriday morning. By early Friday morning the gradient winds will \nbecome stronger and the orientation will shift more E-W, therefore\nexpanding the Wind Advisory to include, southeast Arizona into \nsouthern Gila County and through Pinal, southern Maricopa, and \nYuma Counties. These areas will also be the best corridor for \nstrong winds, where gusts near 40 mph winds can be expected with \nthe peak winds late Friday morning potentially reaching near 50-55\nmph. During peak gusts these same areas will be prone to blowing\ndust, especially in Pinal County.Additionally, while not included\nin the Wind Advisories, areas just north of the advised areas can\nstill expect winds between 25-35 mph Friday and into Saturday \nmorning. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nWinds will continue to be the main weather concern throughout \nSaturday morning with gusts between 30-35 in southern and central \nArizona, but should remain below advisory level winds. However, \nanother round of advisory level winds are expect in Yuma County \nand southern La Paz County Saturday morning, but currently \nallowing the earlier advisory to expire before reissuing to avoid \nconfusion. \n\nAside from the wind concern, guidance is suggesting some lower \nend shower potential over the weekend. The placement of the cut- \noff low to our southwest starting Friday is expected to advect a \ndecent mid and upper level moisture into the area. Some high-based\nlight showers may become possible across southern Arizona as \nearly as Friday evening with best chances Saturday afternoon as \nthe cut-off low weakens and migrates over the region. Unfortunately,\nlittle if any measurable rainfall is expected, as most locations \nwill struggle to get anything more than a trace to a few \nhundredths of an inch. The good news though will be around a 5F-6F\ndecrease in afternoon highs on Saturday, so expect highs in the \nlow 80s across the lower deserts.\n \nBy early next week, weak upper level riding will effect the \nregion, resuming near normal temperatures. Looking out even \nfurther, ensemble guidance is hinting at another deep Pacific \ntrough moving into the Western U.S. early next week and \npotentially reaching our region during the middle part of next \nweek. This next weather system could bring more rain chances and \nanother cool down during the latter half of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1135Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\n\nThe main aviation concern through Friday morning will be the\nincreasingly gusty east winds. Winds this morning will favor the\neast and eventually become gusty late morning with gusts around \n20kts at times. These elevated easterly winds will persist into \ntonight, but with less frequent gusts. By sunrise Friday morning,\neven stronger east winds are expected with gusts at times \nreaching 30 kts by mid Friday morning. Some lofted dust may also \nbecome an issue after sunrise Friday morning, potentially leading \nto some slantwise visibility restrictions. Skies will remain clear\nthrough the period.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nNo major aviation concerns are expected during the next 24 hours\nunder mostly clear skies. Winds this morning will be generally \nlight out of the northwest. By late morning, winds at both \nterminals will switch out of the N/NNE and become breezy with \ngusts 20-25 kt. At KBLH, winds are expected to strength before\nsunrise Friday with occasional northerly gusts between 30-35 kts.\nSkies will remain clear through the period.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDrier air will persist over the region over the next couple of \ndays with mostly clear skies and lowering humidities. MinRHs will\nbe between 15-25% across the western districts and 20-35% for the\neastern districts. Northerly breezy winds will affect southeast \nCA and southwest AZ today before increasing to around advisory \nlevel late tonight and into Friday. The eastern districts will \nkeep overall light winds for most of today before windy \nconditions settle in early Friday morning. Easterly wind gusts as\nhigh as 40-50 mph are expected Friday. Some moisture return \nFriday into Saturday may bring low- end rain chances, but wetting \nrains chances should remain below 10%. MinRH's are expected to\nthen increase to between 25-35% areawide by Sunday.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...     Wind Advisory from 5 AM Friday to 11 AM MST Saturday for AZZ552-\n     553-555-558-560>562.\n\n     Wind Advisory from 8 PM this evening to 2 PM MST Friday for \n     AZZ530.\n\n     Wind Advisory from 5 AM to 3 PM MST Friday for AZZ531-532-536-\n     538-539-554-559.\n\nCA...Wind Advisory from 8 PM this evening to 2 PM PDT Friday for \n     CAZ564-565-569-570.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan\nLONG TERM...Ryan/Kuhlman \nAVIATION...Kuhlman\nFIRE WEATHER...Ryan/Benedict\n",
